<title>RUSSIA: AGROKHLEB-Russia's July grain imports high vs '95.</title>
<headline>AGROKHLEB-Russia's July grain imports high vs '95.</headline>
<dateline>MOSCOW 1996-09-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>The following is a direct contribution from the Agrokhleb Report, published by SovEcon Ltd, an independent research organisation specialising in agricultural production and trade in the former Soviet Union.</p>
<p>RUSSIA'S GRAIN IMPORTS IN JULY, DEFYING EXPECTATIONS AND 1995 LEVELS, STAY HIGH</p>
<p>Normally, with the beginning of a new crop year, Russia's grain imports decline.</p>
<p>However, Russia's imports at the beginning of 1996/97 (July-June) have not dropped at all.</p>
<p>In July, wheat, wheat flour and barley imports were virtually unchanged on June's level.</p>
<p>At the same time, rye imports in July increased significantly month-on-month, the result of massive rye deliveries from Ukraine as the state released its stocks.</p>
<p>As a result, Russia's grain imports this July, particularly of wheat, flour and rye, have jumped greatly on year-ago levels.</p>
<p>This trend reflects the tight grain supply situation in many regions of Russia and low domestic grain sales, as well as high domestic prices, particularly for food grains and flour.</p>
